well now world powers are gathering in  berlin for a new push to secure a  lasting peace deal in libya  in these talks which are led by germany  and the united states  leaders will be discussing their pledges  to withdraw foreign fighters from the  north african country  they also want to ensure the war and  torrent state stays on track toward its  december general elections  libya has been unstable since a  nato-backed uprising toppled long-time  leader muammar gaddafi  in 2011. the oil-rich country was then  divided between a un-supported  government in the capital and rival  authorities based in the country's east  before a transitional government took  power earlier this year  foreign minister heiko maas said that  many challenges still lie ahead in the  pursuit for a stable libya  this is the man muammar gaddafi whose  brutal reign kept libya together  for 40 years in 2011  protests against his rule quickly grew  into a country-wide rebellion  in september 2011 gaddafi was captured  and killed by a militia group  the country soon fell into chaos armed  groups that had been active against his  regime  refused to disband and regional  rivalries  grew elections failed to bring the  country together  and soon rival governments were set up  in the western and eastern parts of the  country  the general national accord in tripoli  was recognized by many western countries  but it had little power over much of the  country  the rival government in tobruk in the  east was  allied with a faction called libyan  national army  led by strongman khalifa haftar  the two sides fought and attended  multiple peace conferences in europe  and continued to fight  meanwhile people smugglers took  advantage of the lawlessness  to use libya as a transit route bringing  migrants and refugees  to libya's mediterranean coast the  european union cut deals with libyan  forces  to try to keep migrants back leaving  hundreds of thousands  stranded in the country  in january 2020 the two warring sides  came to berlin  along with an array of international  leaders to try  to come to an agreement and end foreign  involvement  finally in october 2020 the warring  sides agreed to a ceasefire in all areas  of libya  a u.n resolution helped stabilize the  conflict  by making the berlin agreement legally  binding for its members on the  international level  all in all this led to the formation of  an interim unity government  that's set to stay in place until  elections planned in december of this  year  and for more on that let's bring in now  roderick cusaveta he is  a member of the german parliament's  foreign affairs committee welcome to you  and thank you so much for joining us  i'd just like to begin by asking you  about what we heard about there in that  piece  one of the goals being to secure  elections in december  how realistic are you seeing that given  the complications on the ground right  now  yeah thank you very much indeed we need  to be very sober  whether the election might take place or  not is not clear so far because  there are really different interests  however it is so important that the  german government is continuing its  process  of the berlin talks and that means that  we now  bind in the new transistor transitional  government of libya  which is taking part first time and  several  very important foreign ministers are  present like  foreign minister blinken the foreign  minister of libya but also from some  european states and others so i believe  that there is a good sign to the libyan  population  and second we need to press  those countries who offer mercenaries  military mercenaries  to libya we need to keep the pressure  and try to build up  functioning state structures  guaranteeing security if this  will be the case then elections might  take place  i'd like to ask you a little bit more  about those foreign fighters um that you  just mentioned there because the u.n  warned recently that progress has been  stalled  um that that is actually a prerequisite  also  for elections the the removal of those  foreign fighters from the country  some 20 000 of them are in the country  we understand by  by recent estimates supported by russia  turkey  and others how likely do you see a  meaningful agreement  to make progress on that front  well the german government is holding  background talks  with those countries who are supporting  foreign fighters  but second we could also find an  arrangement  where these mercenaries become part of  the security structure  controlled by united nations for example  as long as the land-owned  security structures are not yet  functioning second  i believe that on the long term we need  the presence  of international engagement for example  of the european union or of the arab  the african union i believe  we should really engage the key leaders  of the african union  that they take over responsibility and  this could be supported by the european  union under the umbrella of the united  nations  this would be a way out which will  guarantee  security on the one side but also needs  acceptance by the libyan population  and if the african union is owning the  process  this would be really a good progress  okay so that that would be one potential  approach there  in the meantime i'd like to ask you  about civilians in all of this  because the u.n is estimating some 1.3  million people are in need of  humanitarian assistance in the in the  country to what extent  is improving the situation for the  libyan people themselves  also on the agenda right now  yeah the libyan population is really  under pressure  but on the other side we have a split of  the national bank  we have a split of the national petrol  system  but on the other side the money is  guaranteed so there is a  is a lot of uh own earned  uh capital inside the country which is  also distributed  to the families nevertheless we have a  strong  need for engagement also of the united  nations  regarding the 